Sure.

Release of a "newer than 3.4.3" version of NetHack
has been so long delayed one grows suspicious that
part of the reason is the lack of sufficient "good
new stuff" to include (not to put down the many
excellent patches I'd like to see in there anyway),
to make a release process seem worth the effort,
in particular some "killer improvement" that would
make "this must be published NOW" be the DevTeam
mindset.

The Sporkhack changes, much as I disagree with many
of them, seem to find resonance with that minority
of NetHack players who are "consistent ascenders"
and frankly bored with the game past some way-point:
quest, castle, or whatever.

That "the whole late game is boring" problem has
been a big blot on NetHack's playability for the
elite players for a very long time now. I'm sure it
is visible well above the horizon for the DevTeam.

Suitably winnowed, the Sporkhack changes could
become that "killer improvement", and so find
themselves being incorporated in the next release of
NetHack.

At that point I'd be playing them (well, if I ever
get that far in the game), so at this point I have
as much vested interest as anyone but Derek Ray
(i.e., damn little but "peanut gallery" chirping
rights) in seeing them "done right".

When innumerate or illogical arguments are advanced
by Derek for why something is "not worth doing
right", which, if you'll review just his postings
about Sporkhack, happens frighteningly often [just
Google for Sporkhack mixed with all the synonyms you
can imagine for "negligible" or "too much trouble"
or "corner case"), then my hackles are raised, both
as a degreed mathematician and as a nearly half
century experienced professional compter programmer
who knows better than to neglect corner cases, and
my natural impulse to comment, forcefully, takes
over, as indeed it should.

